[The complete prime-to-$p$ character index]\label{thm:complete-prime-index}
Let $p\ge5$ and let $e$ be a positive multiple of $p-1$ with $p\nmid e$. Consider complete discretely valued characteristic-zero fields with perfect residue field of characteristic $p$, absolute index $e$, and $\mu_p\subset K$. In the height-two Barsotti--Tate filtration class,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:complete-prime-index}
 \nuMin(p,e,2)=\left\lceil\frac{1+\log_p e}{2}\right\rceil.
\end{equation}
Equivalently, $\nuMin(p,p-1,2)=1$, and for every integer $r\ge2$,
\[
 \nuMin(p,e,2)=r\qquad\text{if}\qquad p^{2r-3}<e<p^{2r-1}.
\]
Put $r=\nuMin(p,e,2)$. The upper statement is uniform over every field in the specified base class: $p^{r+1}$-torsion determines the special prime-to-$p$ inertia character. At every such $e$ there are actual allowed groups over some field with residue $\overline{\F}_p$, isomorphic $p^r$-torsion and different special characters. These lower examples are Barsotti--Tate groups; no assertion that every pair comes from elliptic curves is made.

[Iterated supersingular recovery]\label{thm:ss-iterated-range}
Let $p\ge5$, $n\ge3$, and let $K$ be a complete discretely valued field of characteristic zero with perfect residue field of characteristic $p$, containing $\mu_p$. Put $e=e_K$ and assume $p\nmid e$. If
\begin{equation}\label{eq:ss-iterated-range}
 e<\frac{(p-1)p^{3n-3}}{p^n-1},
\end{equation}
then any isomorphism $G[p^n]\simeq H[p^n]$ between height-two Barsotti--Tate groups with potentially supersingular reduction implies $\theta_G=\theta_H$. Since $(p-1)\mid e$, the numerical condition is equivalently
\[
 e\le(p-1)\bigl(p^{2n-3}+p^{n-3}\bigr).
\]

[Recovery with one canonical quotient]\label{thm:ss-prime-range}
Let $p\ge5$ and $n\ge3$. Let $K$ be a complete discretely valued field of characteristic zero with perfect residue field of characteristic $p$, containing $\mu_p$, and put $e=e_K$. Suppose $p\nmid e$ and
\begin{equation}\label{eq:ss-prime-range}
 e<\frac{(p-1)p^{2n}}{p^n-1}.
\end{equation}
For any two height-two Barsotti--Tate groups $G,H/K$ with potentially supersingular reduction, an isomorphism $G[p^n]\simeq H[p^n]$ implies $\theta_G=\theta_H$.

[Consequences of one canonical quotient]\label{cor:prime-recovery-intro}
For $p\ge5$, a positive multiple $e$ of $p-1$ prime to $p$, and the base class $\mu_p\subset K$, one has
\[
 \nuMin(p,e,2)\le n-1
 \quad\begin{cases}
 n=3,&e<p^3,\\
 n\ge4,&e\le(p-1)(p^n+1).
 \end{cases}
\]
In particular, the following exact values hold:
\begin{align*}
 \nuMin(p,e,2)&=2&&\text{if }p<e<p^3,\\
 \nuMin(p,e,2)&=3&&\text{if }p^3<e\le(p-1)(p^4+1),\\
 \nuMin(p,e,2)&=4&&\text{if }p^5<e\le(p-1)(p^5+1).
\end{align*}
The filtration class and index convention are those fixed above.

[Every tame defect and every level]\label{thm:tame-full-level}
Fix $p\ge5$, $n\ge1$, and $m\in\{2,3,4,6\}$ with $m\mid p+1$. Let $e$ be a positive multiple of $p-1$, and put $s=v_p(e/(p-1))$. There exist a complete discretely valued field $K$ with $e_K=e$ and perfect residue field of characteristic $p$, and an elliptic curve over $K$ with full rational $p^n$-torsion and exact semistability defect $m$, if and only if
\begin{equation}\label{eq:tame-full-criterion}
 s\ge n-1,\qquad
 e\ge\frac{p^2-1}{m}\,
       p^{\max\{2n-2,\,4n-4-s\}}.
\end{equation}
Every permitted index is attained with residue field $\overline{\F}_p$.
The same assertion holds for $p=3$ and specified tame defect $m=2$ or $4$. The wild characteristic-three defects are treated separately in Theorem~\ref{thm:three-range}.

\label{thm:three-range}
Let $e=2u$ be a positive even integer, put $s=v_3(u)$, and let $n\ge1$. For each $m\in\{3,6,12\}$, an elliptic curve with additive reduction, exact semistability defect $m$, and full rational $3^n$-torsion exists over some complete discretely valued field of absolute ramification $e$ and perfect residue field if and only if
\begin{equation}\label{eq:wild-criterion}
 s\ge n-1,\qquad
 me\ge 8\,3^{\max\{2n-2,\,4n-4-s\}}.
\end{equation}
The field can be chosen with residue field $\overline{\F}_3$.

Allowing every nontrivial defect, the largest possible level is
\begin{equation}\label{eq:maximum-three}
 N(e)=1+\min\left\{s,\left\lfloor\frac{L+1}{2}\right\rfloor,
                         \left\lfloor\frac{L+s+1}{4}\right\rfloor\right\},
 \qquad L=\lfloor\log_3u\rfloor.
\end{equation}
Among potentially good elliptic curves, full rational $3^{N(e)+1}$-torsion forces good reduction, and that level is least.

\label{thm:cyclic-bound}
Let $p\ge5$, let $R$ be a complete discrete valuation ring with algebraically closed residue field of characteristic $p$, and put $a=v_R(p)$ and $v=v_p(a)$. If a one-dimensional formal group of special height two has an $R$-rational point of exact order $p^n$, with $n\ge2$, then
\begin{equation}\label{eq:cyclic-bound}
 a>(p^2-1)p^{2n-3-v}.
\end{equation}
A negative exponent on the right is interpreted in $\Q_{>0}$.

\label{thm:cyclotomic-exact}
Let $p\ge3$, and suppose that $K$ has algebraically closed residue field, $e_K=p(p-1)$, $\mu_p\subset K$, and $\mu_{p^2}\not\subset K$. Choose a Cohen coefficient field $K_0\subset K$, put $K_1=K_0(\mu_p)$, and write $d=v_K(\mathfrak D_{K/K_1})$. The last upper ramification break of $K(\mu_{p^3})/K$ is exactly
\begin{equation}\label{eq:exact-second-break}
 \delta_2=(p-1)(2p+1)-d\ge p(p-1).
\end{equation}

\label{thm:cyclotomic-bound}
Let $p\ge3$. Suppose that $K$ has algebraically closed residue field, $e_K=p(p-1)$, $\mu_p\subset K$, and $\mu_{p^2}\not\subset K$. Then $C=K(\mu_{p^3})$ is cyclic of degree $p^2$ over $K$. If $\delta_1<\delta_2$ are its upper ramification breaks, in the classical convention without a conductor shift, then
\begin{equation}\label{eq:cyclotomic-bound}
 \delta_1+p\delta_2\ge p(p^2-p-1).
\end{equation}
Consequently $\delta_1\le p-1$ implies
\begin{equation}\label{eq:second-break}
 \delta_2\ge p^2-p-2+\frac1p.
\end{equation}

\label{tf-lem:inertia}
Let $p$ be odd, and let $E/K$ be potentially good with nontrivial tame semistability defect $m$ and full rational $p$-torsion. Its good special fibre is supersingular. If $p\ge5$, every nontrivial potentially good defect is tame and belongs to $\{2,3,4,6\}$; for $p=3$ a nontrivial tame defect belongs to $\{2,4\}$.

After completed maximal unramified base change, let $L/K$ be the minimal good extension. There is an integral formal parameter $t$ and a residue class $r\in(\Z/m\Z)^\times$ such that every nonzero rational descended $p$-power torsion point satisfies
\begin{equation}\label{tf-eq:congruence}
 v_L(t(P))\equiv r\pmod m.
\end{equation}

\label{tf-lem:extrema}
Let $F$ be a one-dimensional formal group of special height two over a complete mixed-characteristic DVR, with full rational $p^n$-torsion. The residue field may be imperfect. Normalize the valuation to $\Z$, put $E=v(p)$, and let $a\ge b>0$ be the largest and smallest nonzero $p$-torsion valuations. Then
\begin{equation}\label{tf-eq:mass}
 M:=\frac{E}{p-1}=a+pb.
\end{equation}
If $b_n,A_n$ are the minimum and maximum exact $p^n$-torsion valuations, then
\begin{equation}\label{tf-eq:extrema}
 b_n=\frac b{p^{2n-2}},\qquad
 A_n=\frac{M-p^{c+1}b}{p^{2n-2-c}},\qquad
 c=\min\left\{n-1,\left\lfloor\log_p\frac{M}{(p+1)b}\right\rfloor\right\}.
\end{equation}

\label{tf-lem:surface}
Let $k=\overline{\F}_p$, and let $m$ be one of the allowed tame defects. There is a complete regular local $W(k)$-algebra $R_n$ of dimension two, flat over $W(k)$, carrying an actual good elliptic scheme with a full Drinfeld $p^n$-level structure. It has an integral formal parameter $t$ such that the coordinates $x=t(P)$ and $y=t(Q)$ of a labelled basis form a regular system of parameters. A faithful semilinear $C_m$-action fixes the level labels and $W(k)$ and satisfies
\begin{equation}\label{tf-eq:scalar-action}
 \gamma x=\zeta x,\qquad\gamma y=\zeta y,
\end{equation}
where $\zeta\in W(k)$ has exact order $m$. Its special elliptic action has exact order $m$.

\label{tf-lem:realization}
Fix $n\ge1$ and an allowed tame defect $m$. For every integer $A\ge1$ with $A\equiv1\pmod m$, there is an elliptic curve with full rational $p^n$-torsion and exact defect $m$ over a complete field with residue $\overline{\F}_p$ and absolute index $E/m$, where
\begin{equation}\label{tf-eq:index-A}
 \frac{E}{p-1}=p^{2n-2-c}(A+p^{2c+1}).
\end{equation}
Here $0\le c<n-1$ when $p^{2c}\le A\le p^{2c+2}$, and $c=n-1$ when $A\ge p^{2n-2}$. Adjacent expressions agree at their common endpoints.

\label{lem:valuation-sum}
Let $R$ be a complete discrete valuation ring with algebraically closed residue field of characteristic three. Suppose that a one-dimensional formal group of height two has full rational $3^n$-torsion over $\operatorname{Frac}(R)$. Write $d=v_R(3)$, and let $A$ and $B$ be the largest and smallest valuations of primitive $3^n$-torsion coordinates. Then $A\ge B\ge1$ and
\begin{equation}\label{eq:primitive-sum}
 d=2\,3^{n-1}\left(A+3^nB+
       \sum_{j=1}^{n-1}2\,3^{n-j-1}\min(A,3^{2j}B)\right).
\end{equation}
If $0\le r\le n-1$ and $3^{2r}B\le A\le3^{2r+2}B$, with no upper condition when $r=n-1$, then
\begin{equation}\label{eq:regional-sum}
 d=2\,3^{2n-r-2}(A+3^{2r+1}B).
\end{equation}

\label{lem:wild-arc}
Let $n\ge1$ and let $A\ge1$ be divisible by three. Set
\begin{equation}\label{eq:arc-degree}
 d=2\,3^{n-1}\left(A+3^n+
            \sum_{j=1}^{n-1}2\,3^{n-j-1}\min(A,3^{2j})\right).
\end{equation}
There is a complete discrete valuation ring $R$ with residue field $\overline{\F}_3$, $v_R(3)=d$, and a good supersingular elliptic curve over $R$ with full rational $3^n$-torsion and primitive valuations having extrema $A,1$.

The ring and marked curve admit a faithful semilinear $C_3$-action preserving every marked point. They admit such an action of $C_6$ if $A$ is odd, and of $C_3\rtimes C_4$ if $A\equiv1\pmod4$. The order-four generator acts by inversion on $C_3$. All actions are trivial on the residue field and induce the faithful indicated automorphisms of $y^2=x^3-x$.

\label{lem:inverse-polygons}
Let $R$ and $F$ satisfy the hypotheses of Theorem~\ref{thm:cyclic-bound}. There is an integral coordinate and a deformation parameter $u\in\mathfrak m_R$ such that, writing $a=v_R(p)$, $b=v_R(u)$, and $q=p^2$, the nonzero $p$-torsion valuations are as follows. If $b\ge ap/(p+1)$, all have valuation $a/(q-1)$. Otherwise they have valuations
\begin{equation}\label{eq:alpha-beta}
 \alpha=\frac{a-b}{p-1}\quad(p-1\text{ points}),\qquad
 \beta=\frac{b}{p(p-1)}\quad(q-p\text{ points}).
\end{equation}
Here $b=+\infty$ is permitted when $u=0$.

If $z$ is a nonzero torsion point of valuation $0<Z<a$, its $p$-preimages have valuations
\begin{equation}\label{eq:inverse-polygons}
 \begin{cases}
 Z/q & b\ge Z(p-1)/p,\\[2pt]
 (Z-b)/p\text{ with multiplicity }p,
 \quad\beta\text{ with multiplicity }q-p
 & b<Z(p-1)/p.
 \end{cases}
\end{equation}
The equality case belongs to the first line. Once an inverse chain takes a step of the first kind, or a step to $\beta$, all its later inverse steps divide the valuation by $q$.

\label{prop:mixed-obstruction}
Let $p\ge5$, let $e=e_K$ and $v=v_p(e)$. Let $M/K$ be a height-two Barsotti--Tate group with potentially supersingular good reduction. Let $H/K$ have a two-step filtration by potentially good height-one groups whose dimensions sum to one. If
$M[p^n]\simeq H[p^n]$ for $n\ge2$, then
\begin{equation}\label{eq:mixed-obstruction}
 e>p^{2n-3-v}.
\end{equation}

\label{lem:actual-quotients}
The quotient by the high line has parameter $pB$ if $B<e$ and $s-B$ if $B>e$; at $B=e$ its first-level polygon has one slope and every nonzero point has valuation $e$. If the first-level module is rational and $J$ is an inertia-stable low line, the quotient by $J$ has parameter $B/p$. A one-slope group has first-level value $e$, and its quotient by any stable line has parameter $e$, high value $pe$, and low value $e/p$.

These are good formal quotients over $L$ with the same finite special character. A comparison at level $p^n$ preserving the chosen lines induces a comparison of their quotients at level $p^{n-1}$.

\label{rc-cor:strictordering}
If two compatible residual representations have two-slope parameters with
$p\nmid h_1$ and $p\mid h_2$, then $h_2<h_1$.

\label{rc-prop:oneslopecyclic}
If a one-slope supersingular group has a $G_K$-stable cyclic subgroup of
order $p^n$, then $v_p(e_K)\geq n-1$.

\label{fc-prop:oneslope}
Suppose \(p\nmid E\) and \(G[p^2]\simeq H[p^2]\) over \(L\). If \(G\)
is one-slope, then \(H\) is one-slope.

\label{fc-cor:Aupper}
For \(p=5\) and \(e_K=20\), two potentially supersingular height-two
groups with isomorphic \(125\)-torsion have equal special inertia traces.

\label{thm:three-family-reduction}
Let $p\ge5$, $n\ge3$, $p\nmid e$, $(p-1)\mid e$, and $e<p^n$. Let two potentially supersingular height-two groups over the same complete field $K$ have isomorphic $p^n$-torsion and unequal special inertia traces. After completed unramified base change, let $L/K$ be their common tame good field of degree $p^2-1$. Suppose both good models have two Newton slopes. Write their Hasse valuations as $h_i=(p-1)z_i$, normalized by $v_L(L^\times)=\Z$, and let $\alpha_i$ be their tangent descent scalars for a fixed generator of $\operatorname{Gal}(L/K)$. After interchanging the models if necessary, one of the following holds:
\begin{align}
 z_1&=(p-1)p^n+b,&z_2&=p^{n-1}b,
 &p+1\le b\le p^2-p-1,\quad p\nmid b;
 \label{eq:pg-family-one}\\
 z_1&=b+(p-1)p^{n-1}\ell,&z_2&=p^nb,
 &1\le b\le p-1,\quad b+1\le\ell\le p+1.
 \label{eq:pg-family-two}
\end{align}
The third possibility is
\begin{equation}\label{eq:pg-family-sign}
 z_1=z_2=\frac{p-1}{2}p^n,\qquad \alpha_2=-\alpha_1,
\end{equation}
with the further necessary congruence
\begin{equation}\label{eq:pg-family-congruence}
 (p-1)p^{n-2}\mid(p+1)e-\frac{p-1}{2}(p^n+1).
\end{equation}
In this third case the residual representations split into distinct characters, and the given isomorphism interchanges the canonical residual lines with the opposite character lines. Every family is still subject to the strict two-slope inequalities $0<z_i<pe$.

[Primitive presentation]\label{pg-lem:primitives}
Let $B$ be a finite-dimensional commutative Hopf algebra over $k$, generated by primitive elements $X,Y$. The space
\[
 V=\operatorname{span}_k\{X,X^p,X^{p^2},\ldots,Y,Y^p,Y^{p^2},\ldots\}
\]
is its full primitive space, and $\dim_k B=p^{\dim_k V}$.

[Minimum torsion values and leading characters]\label{pg-lem:scaling}
Put $D=p^{2n-1}$ and choose $r$ with $rD\equiv1\pmod m$. For model $i$, the minimum nonzero $p^n$-torsion coordinate value is $z_i/D$, attained by $(p-1)D$ points. The rescaled graph has an additive special fibre whose primitive space has dimension $2n$. It carries a $\Z/D\Z$ grading in which $X^{p^j},Y^{p^j}$ have respective weights $-z_1p^j,-z_2p^j$. The tame scalars
\begin{equation}\label{pg-eq:lambda}
 \lambda_i=\alpha_i\zeta^{-rz_i}
\end{equation}
belong to $\F_p^\times$, and no Frobenius iterate of either coordinate is zero. Moreover,
\begin{equation}\label{pg-eq:bounds}
 (p-1)p^{n-1}<z_i<pe<p^{n+1},\qquad s_i\le n.
\end{equation}

\label{pg-prop:chains}
If $\lambda_1\ne\lambda_2$, then $s_1=s_2=n$. In every case $\max(s_1,s_2)\ge n-1$. If $\lambda_1=\lambda_2$, order $s=s_1\le s_2=t$ and write $z_1=p^s a$, $z_2=p^t b$ with $p\nmid ab$. Then $a\ne b$ and
\begin{equation}\label{pg-eq:chain-congruence}
 (p-1)p^{\max(0,2n-1-s-t)}\mid a-b.
\end{equation}

[A lower-level congruence]\label{pg-lem:lower-level}
For an unequal-type two-slope comparison at level $p^N$, $N\ge1$, suppose the normalized Hasse values are $z$ and $p^t b$, with $p\nmid zb$. No bound on $e$ is needed. Then
\begin{equation}\label{pg-eq:lower-level}
 (p-1)p^{\max(0,2N-1-t)}\mid z-b.
\end{equation}

\label{lem:terminal-plane-refinement}
Suppose $B=pb$ with $p\nmid b$ and the full $p^3$-division field $F/L$ has degree $p^4$. In a basis with $H=\F_pe_1$, the terminal space
\[
 V_2=\{C\in M_2(\F_p):1+p^2C\in\operatorname{Gal}(F/L)\}
\]
has dimension two. Every $C\in V_2$ has $C_{21}=0$, and the kernel of $C\mapsto C_{22}$ is exactly $\F_pE_{12}$.

\label{prop:exact-third-point-profiles}
One can choose a common basis $Q_1,Q_2$ of the actual $125$-torsion with $25Q_i=e_i$ and point fields stable under a tame lift. Set $P_i=5Q_i$, $E_i=L(P_i)$ and $E_i^+=L(Q_i)$. Then $E_i^+/E_i$ is Galois of degree $25$, and
\[
\begin{array}{c|c|r|r}
 &\text{lower breaks}&\text{diagonal quotient}&\text{off-diagonal quotient}\\\hline
 E_1^+/E_1&(606,6936)&1872&606\\
 E_2^+/E_2&(624,6834)&624&1866.
\end{array}
\]
The directions are the actual first-level translations on the two residual axes.
